<p>Dance is the art of the moving human body. Its medium is not paint or stone or sound but the body itself, and its subject matter is motion, feeling, and the states of mind that the body can express more directly than words. A dancer's gesture can communicate grief, joy, conflict, or tenderness with an immediacy that no other art form matches.</p>
<p>Unit 10 covers the major forms of dance: ritual dance, court dance, ballet, modern dance, and popular dance. It also examines the formal elements that give dance its structure: repetition, balance, spatial design, and the relationship between movement and music. The unit traces the evolution from ballet's stylized vocabulary (arabesque, bourrée, relevé) to modern dance's return to natural, expressive movement.</p>
<p>This unit asks you to watch dance with the same kind of attention you have been giving to paintings and sculptures. The work moves and then it is gone. Training your eye to follow that movement and describe what you saw is one of the more demanding skills this course teaches.</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Learning Objectives</p>
<p>After completing this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Distinguish between ritual dance, ballet, modern dance, and popular dance</li>
<li>Identify the formal elements of dance: repetition, balance, spatial design, and rhythm</li>
<li>Explain how modern dance differs from classical ballet in both technique and philosophy</li>
<li>Describe the relationship between dance and music</li>
<li>Apply descriptive and interpretive criticism to a dance performance</li>
</ul>




